  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 253 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nSunday Night through Saturday:\n\nSynopsis: Drier air will lead to pleasant and comfortable conditions \non Monday afternoon. Moisture will gradually work back into southern \nWisconsin Tuesday through Wednesday, leading to muggier conditions. \nA cold front will swing through the western Great Lakes Tuesday \nevening into Wednesday, triggering the next round of shower and \nthunderstorm development as it impinges upon the returning humid air \nmass. Tuesday evening & night currently appears to be most favored \nfor storms in southern Wisconsin. Broader ingredients will be in \nplace for a few stronger storms with heavy downpours, though where \nexactly those ingredients come together best remains uncertain. Will \ncontinue to monitor trends. If the front lingers across the region \nlong enough, additional storm development would be possible on \nWednesday afternoon. Passing high pressure and drier air will lead \nto pleasant & largely precip-free conditions through the end of the \nweek. An isolated shower is possible on Friday as broad warm \nadvection spreads over the Upper Midwest, though confidence in \noccurrence, timing, and placement is low at this time.\n\nTuesday Evening into Wednesday: Continue to anticipate a window of \nstorm potential along a passing cold front. Global deterministic & \nensemble guidance remains in sound agreement regarding an \nevening/overnight frontal passage, which continues to point toward \nthis time frame as being the most likely to see storm development. \nGiven the model agreement, continue to maintain precip probabilities \nfrom the NBM, which maximize (~75+%) during the ~7 PM Tuesday - 7 AM \nWednesday time frame. Favorable positioning of a seasonably strong \nupper jet/attendant wind shear over the Upper Mississippi Valley \npoints toward organized storm potential, though where exactly the \nbest moisture/instability overlap with the favorable dynamics \nremains to be seen. Global ensembles currently depict southern \nWisconsin on the northeast side of an instability plume centered \nover Iowa, which if verified would generally place the highest \nsevere potential from the western half of the CWA back westward. Any \nchanges in frontal timing, however, would lead to shifts in the \ninstability plume and zone(s) of greatest severe potential. \nSeasonably moist conditions will be supportive for heavy downpours, \nthough whether enough storms and/or training occurs to support a \nflooding threat remains to be seen. Will thus be monitoring trends \nregarding the aforementioned uncertainties over the next couple \ndays. \n\nQuigley",
